  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Near record warmth today with dry weather continuing \nthrough late afternoon.\n\nA mid-level ridge will continue to build east across the Atlantic \nseaboard through mid-week, supporting surface high pressure to \npersist off the eastern Atlantic. Overall this will support a \ncontinuation of dry weather through late this afternoon with periods \nof mid/high clouds drifting across the region.\n\nA belt of 25-30 knot southerly flow in the boundary layer will bring \nsome increase in winds today, most notably along the lee slopes of \nthe Chautauqua Ridge this morning where downslope winds may gust to \n30 mph at times.\n\nAs the upper ridge builds further north Wednesday, 850mb \ntemperatures will continue to rise, peaking between +14C to +15C. \nThe anomalous warmth aloft will combine with some filtered sunshine, \nmechanical mixing from the southerly breeze, and downslope \ncompressional warming to boost temperatures into the mid to upper \n70s across lower elevations. See Climate section for more \ninformation regarding record temperatures. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers return tonight, with unsettled weather and \ncooler temperatures lasting through next weekend.  \n\nDeep troughing and associated closed low will move into the Great \nLakes for the later half of the work week. A deep southerly flow \noriginating from the Gulf and southern Atlantic Ocean will support a \nplume of moisture into the eastern Great Lakes region tonight into \nThursday morning. This deep moisture combined with forcing from the \nsurface to 500mb due to a passing cold front will support widespread \nshowers across the region, and provide some modest relief to the \nrecent dry spell. Rainfall amounts will not be overly impressive \nhowever given the fast motion of the system and narrow axis of \nmoisture. Most areas will see 0.25\" or less of rain tonight.\n\nIn the wake of the cold front, expect gusty southwest winds \nnortheast of Lake Erie with gusts of 25 to 35 mph impacting much of \nthe Niagara Frontier on Thursday. \n\nThe trough will move overhead Friday and persist into the weekend, \npossibly evolving into a slow moving mid level low by late Friday \nthrough Saturday over the eastern Great Lakes. The trough and \nassociated forcing and moisture will support a chance of showers \nFriday into the weekend. Ongoing cold advection will also bring a \nnotable downward trend in temperatures, with highs this weekend back \nto near average after the exceptional warmth this week.",
